@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer

@* FR-71: the rule between two days of one conversation.

   Chat keeps its whole history, so a conversation is read as a run of days rather than as one list.
   Without this a line from March is read as one from this morning - the bubble's own meta carries
   the date, but a reader scanning a column of messages is not reading meta lines.

   Its own component rather than a block inside the screen's loop, and for the same reason ChatLine
   is one: the conversation arrives over a live hub connection, so a statically rendered screen never
   reaches a state with two days in it and nothing in this solution could read a separator back.
   Given an instant and the present, this renders on its own (DW-17, DW-18).

   `role="presentation"`, because this <li> is a heading over the conversation and not a line of it.
   The list around it is the message list, and an unqualified <li> would be counted as one - a screen
   reader announcing "list, nine items" over a conversation of six messages and three days. The role
   drops this element's own listitem semantics and keeps its contents in the tree, so the day is
   still read out where it stands.

   Both instants are parameters rather than readings of a clock here. `Now` is read once per render
   by the screen and handed down: read per branch, a midnight ticking between two of the three
   questions below renders a separator carrying two labels or none at all.

   Independent @if blocks and one literal localizer key per string, for the reason the screens
   state: an `else` at markup level reads to NFR-14's scan as a rendered English word. *@

<li class="dt-chat-day" role="presentation">
    @if (ChatViews.NamesTheDayAsToday(SentAt, Now))
    {
        <span class="dt-chat-day__label">@Localizer["ChatToday"]</span>
    }
    @if (ChatViews.NamesTheDayAsYesterday(SentAt, Now))
    {
        <span class="dt-chat-day__label">@Localizer["ChatYesterday"]</span>
    }
    @* Everything older than yesterday says which day it was. *@
    @if (ChatViews.NamesTheDayByDate(SentAt, Now))
    {
        <span class="dt-chat-day__label">@Date</span>
    }
</li>

@code {
    /// <summary>When the first line under this separator was sent.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public DateTimeOffset SentAt { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The present the day is named against, read once per render by the screen that owns the clock.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public DateTimeOffset Now { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The date this separator carries, in the culture the application runs in (NFR-15). Date only:
    /// the clock reading is on every bubble below it, and a separator repeating one of them would be
    /// a heading naming a single line rather than the day it opens.
    /// </summary>
    private string Date =>
        SentAt.ToLocalTime().ToString("d", System.Globalization.CultureInfo.CurrentCulture);
}
